Exact lowering/runtime cause:
parse.zag:802 converts recognized bare constants into nullary calls; parse_const at 867 onward desugars const declarations into nullary functions. Imported constants can arrive as identifiers instead. Stable ARM64 identifier lowering materializes the ordinary fat-function descriptor {code_ptr, env_ptr} and stores its address as the cursor value instead of evaluating the accessor. In fresh original V73 disassembly at 0x100015db4, adr constructs the code address, stores it at x29-0x80, stores zero at x29-0x78, then stores the descriptor address in cursor x29-0x70 at 0x100015ddc. The caller passes &at at 0x100015e2c; the pointer identifies the correct slot, whose value is wrong. checkpoint.zag:18 correctly refuses the nonsensical i32 cursor with cl_bound()=2002. V73 maps that first section failure to 81. This is not a six-slice ABI failure, pointer alias failure, packet ceiling failure or digest failure.

The repaired V73 emits bl 0x1000108c4 at the same initializer address and stores the returned value in x29-0x70. Original/current disassembly outputs are pinned. The independent no-checkpoint bare_value() probe repeats the wrong value while explicit access returns 112, removing caller and write complexity.

Current local compiler source already marks _zag_desugared_const in parse.zag:886 and recognizes it in native/acodegen.zag:1098, returns the accessor type at 1738 and emits the nullary call at 2171. Full files and their origin hashes are frozen in compiler_source_witness. This source behavior is absent from the stable executable's measured lowering. Source presence is not evidence of executable build provenance. No compiler file was patched by this agent, no candidate was built, and no bootstrap/fixpoint or broad compiler regression claim is made.

